**World Diabetes Day** is on **Saturday 14 November 2026**. This year's theme is **"Early action on diabetes"**. It focuses on **earlier diagnosis**, access to good care and **reducing the stigma** that can stop people asking for help.

Acting early really matters. In **type 1 diabetes**, spotting the signs quickly can **save a life**. In **type 2 diabetes**, finding it early, or even before it starts, can **prevent serious problems** with the eyes, nerves, kidneys, heart and feet.

{% callout type="danger" title="Get help the same day" %}
If you or your child have signs of type 1 diabetes, see a GP **the same day** or call **NHS 111**. Go to A&E or call **999** if there is also **being sick, tummy pain, breath that smells fruity, deep or fast breathing, or drowsiness**. These can be signs of **diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)**, which is life-threatening.
{% /callout %}

## Early signs of type 1 diabetes: the 4 Ts

Type 1 diabetes usually starts in **childhood or young adulthood**, but it can start at **any age**. Symptoms can come on **quickly, over days or weeks**.

Diabetes UK uses the **4 Ts** to help people spot it:

- **Toilet:** peeing more, especially at night, or **bedwetting in a child who was dry**
- **Thirsty:** being very thirsty
- **Tired:** feeling very tired
- **Thinner:** losing weight without trying

Other signs include **blurred vision**, **thrush** and **cuts that heal slowly**.

If type 1 diabetes is suspected, you will usually be **referred to a specialist team the same day**, because treatment with insulin needs to start quickly. Read more about [type 1 diabetes](/conditions/metabolic-and-hormonal/type-1-diabetes/).

{% callout type="info" title="Trust your instincts" %}
If your child is suddenly drinking much more, going to the toilet a lot or losing weight, do not wait to see if it passes. See a GP the same day. A simple finger-prick or blood test can show high blood sugar.
{% /callout %}

## Early signs of type 2 diabetes

Type 2 diabetes is the most common type. About **9 in 10 people** with diabetes in the UK have type 2. It often develops **slowly**, and **many people have no symptoms at first**. That is why checks are so important.

Signs can include:

- **peeing more**, especially at night
- feeling **very thirsty**
- feeling **tired**
- **losing weight** without trying
- **itching** around the genitals, or repeated **thrush**
- **cuts that heal slowly**
- **blurred vision**

See a GP if you have any of these, or if you are worried.

### Are you at higher risk?

You are more likely to get type 2 diabetes if you:

- are **over 40**, or **over 25** if you are of **South Asian** heritage
- have a **parent, brother or sister** with diabetes
- are **overweight**, especially around the waist
- are of **South Asian, Chinese, Black African or African-Caribbean** heritage
- have **high blood pressure**
- had **diabetes in pregnancy** (gestational diabetes)

You can check your risk in a few minutes with the free **Diabetes UK Know Your Risk** tool. In England, adults aged **40 to 74** without a heart or circulation condition are also invited for a free **NHS Health Check** every 5 years.

## Prediabetes: the chance to act early

A blood test called **HbA1c** shows your average blood sugar over the last 2 to 3 months.

| HbA1c | What it usually means |
| --- | --- |
| below 42 mmol/mol | Normal |
| 42 to 47 mmol/mol | Prediabetes (higher risk) |
| 48 mmol/mol or higher | Type 2 diabetes |

**Prediabetes** means your blood sugar is higher than it should be, but not high enough to be type 2 diabetes. This is the best time to act. **Healthy changes can stop it turning into type 2 diabetes.**

In England, you may be offered a place on the free **NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme**, which can help stop prediabetes becoming type 2 diabetes.

For some people who already have type 2 diabetes, **losing weight**, especially soon after diagnosis, can put it into **remission**. This means blood sugar stays in a healthy range without diabetes medicine. Read more about [type 2 diabetes](/conditions/metabolic-and-hormonal/type-2-diabetes/).

## Already have diabetes? Act early on complications

Acting early is not just about diagnosis. Everyone with diabetes should have these checks **at least once a year**, to catch problems early:

- **HbA1c** blood test
- **eye screening**
- **foot check**
- **kidney** tests
- **blood pressure** and **cholesterol**

If you have not had your checks this year, ask your GP practice.

## Help with costs

- **Prescriptions:** in England, if your diabetes is treated with medicine, you can get a **medical exemption certificate**, which makes **all** your NHS prescriptions free. In Wales, prescriptions are free for everyone. Read about [free NHS prescriptions](/support/health-costs/prescriptions/).
- **Eye tests:** people with diabetes can get **free NHS sight tests**. Read about [free eye tests](/support/health-costs/eye-care/).
- **Benefits:** if diabetes affects your daily life, you may be able to get PIP, or DLA for a child. Read [type 1 diabetes, work and benefits](/conditions/metabolic-and-hormonal/type-1-diabetes/benefits-and-work/) and [type 2 diabetes, work and benefits](/conditions/metabolic-and-hormonal/type-2-diabetes/benefits-and-work/).

## Five ways to act early this World Diabetes Day

1. **Learn the 4 Ts** and share them with family and friends, especially parents.
2. **Check your risk** of type 2 diabetes with the Diabetes UK Know Your Risk tool.
3. **Book a check** with your GP or go to your NHS Health Check if you are invited.
4. **Keep up with your yearly checks** if you already have diabetes.
5. **Talk about it.** Reducing stigma helps people ask for help sooner.
